./LOGBOOK.md:650:### 1345 — M2 candidate readiness and M3 validator registration are explicit (TASK-260715-30lv40)
./LOGBOOK.md:652:- M3 SEAM: After current `BaseReady`, M3 may emit one local single-use validation-start event per transport attempt. M2 allocates the exact captured tuple in finite `reconnectValidator` phase before resources/callbacks, returns one candidate success or finite failure, and owns no reconnect retry. T26 alone publishes full; T27 clears registration before degraded publication.
./LOGBOOK.md:653:- PROJECTION/DIAGRAMS: Public Full/Degraded predicates are target-state truth rather than circular source guards. Reasserting and every candidate/validator phase remain all-false. The lifecycle diagram now distinguishes M3 Awaiting Base from registered Validating Relay; the ownership view remains authority-only.
./LOGBOOK.md:658:- RETRY/REASON: T15 is the sole same-runtime re-entry after exhaustion or a local-change-required relay result. It accepts only a higher locally authorized relay-only policy generation on the current healthy base, allocates a fresh bounded recovery cycle, and cannot accept remote/UI input or overlap M3 path/host/route/lane/sleep/NAT64/captive ownership. Successful startup activation publishes local finite `activation_ready` with all capabilities false until atomic full publication changes the reason to `none`.
./LOGBOOK.md:667:### 1205 — M2 capability truth separates relay reprobe from M3 reconnect (TASK-260715-30lv40)
./LOGBOOK.md:669:- OWNERSHIP: M2 exclusively owns relay bootstrap/session loss and one bounded relay-only reprobe while the current base stays healthy; it remains publicly connected/degraded and never sets provider reasserting. M3 exclusively owns path, host, route, mandatory/lane-pool, sleep/wake, NAT64, and captive reconnect. A current M3 event cancels M2 timers/attempts before any M3 retry loop begins.
./LOGBOOK.md:670:- GENERATION/PRIVACY: Runtime generation remains M1-owned, transport generation is M3-owned, and relay attempt/active generation is M2-owned. Association identity cannot cross that tuple. Schema-2 runtime/capability snapshots add finite reasons, retry owner, generations, and locally validated build/limit evidence while preserving explicit schema-1 responses; remote text and sensitive traffic identifiers remain structurally excluded.
./LOGBOOK.md:729:- RESOURCE: Adapter capacity is an atomic non-waiting reservation bounded by the HEV session ceiling and the measured M0/SSH/Core memory equation; exact flow, chunk, queue, window, and timeout values remain injected measurement inputs for M1/M3 rather than promoted constants. Diagnostics are aggregate schema-v1 only and exclude credentials, SOCKS bytes, endpoints, hostnames, payloads, and flow identifiers.
./LOGBOOK.md:900:- NOTE for M3 (G1/G3): effective end-to-end mapping lifetime is min(client 60 s, relay 120 s) = 60 s — G3 must judge that effective value, not the relay timer alone; the 1472 "unfragmented egress" arm is IPv4 arithmetic (IPv6 would be 1452), the HEV bound is load-bearing.
./LOGBOOK.md:904:- DECISION: M0 uses fixed schema constants and unilateral local caps; nothing new on the wire beyond the existing `maxFrame` min-negotiation. Hello flag bit 1, feature bit 1, and message types `0x40–0x4F` reserved (unallocated) for an evidence-gated M3 limits exchange. Rationale: peer-advertised limits are unverifiable claims (local hard caps stay mandatory regardless), the datagram ceiling is HEV-pinned so negotiation has nothing to negotiate, and manifest-verified relay upload version-locks peer pairs. Full record: board outcome `TASK-260715-18owh7_decision.md`; awaiting review acceptance (proposed ADR-021).
./LOGBOOK.md:908:- STATUS: Final numeric tuning is an explicit M3 evidence gate (STORY-260715-1zzt0c, STORY-260715-19ii11; gates G1–G4 in decision §7).
./LOGBOOK.md:941:- VERIFICATION: Reviewer reran independently on the exact tree: `make validate-native` exit 0 (iOS device/sim + macOS provider/harness matrix, 110 Swift tests), `make validate-core` exit 0, `make validate-libssh2` real rekey/KEX exit 0. `otool -l` confirmed every slice: HEV ios/tvos minos 18.0, macos 15.0; ReluxLibSSH2 ios 18.0, macos 15.0 — no 10.14/11.0 records remain.
./LOGBOOK.md:949:- VERIFICATION: `make validate-native` passed the complete iOS device, iOS Simulator, macOS provider/harness and stripped-link audit matrix, followed by 110 Swift tests and `swift build`. `make validate-core`, `make validate-libssh2` (real rekey/KEX/global-request test), native negative gates, strict Swift format, Python compile, shell syntax, JSON parsing, and `git diff --check` passed.
./LOGBOOK.md:975:- EXPECTATION FIX: A stalled SOCKS peer does not guarantee Darwin endpoint-B backpressure because native HEV may continue draining the tunnel descriptor. The stall test now asserts the deterministic contract (`sent + would-block drops + ENOBUFS drops == received`) and allows the legitimate zero-drop schedule instead of forcing timing-dependent loss.
./LOGBOOK.md:1003:- ROUTING: This is the third fork-gap round anticipated by TASK-260720-2sltje. Do not add a partial/mock-only adapter. Recommend failing libssh2 red at M0 unless its value justifies one explicitly approved consolidated fork extension for manual windows, typed open failures, and lossless exec-exit metadata; the alternative is an explicit M0-to-M3/common-contract revision. Exact evidence and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_third-public-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:1011:### 1145 — libssh2 adapter remains blocked on server-rekey observability and keepalive replies (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:1012:- BLOCKER: The accepted `libssh2_session_rekey` fork clears client byte/time/manual trigger initiation only. The exact public header exports no KEX lifecycle callback or generation query. Pinned libssh2 handles inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` entirely inside private `packet.c`/`transport.c` state, so the adapter cannot truthfully emit `.serverInitiated`, mark `activeKeyExchange`, increment generation, or distinguish server-KEX success/failure through the public boundary.
./LOGBOOK.md:1014:- EVIDENCE: The public macOS XCFramework exports only `libssh2_session_rekey`, `libssh2_keepalive_config`, and `libssh2_keepalive_send` among relevant symbols. `keepalive.c:52-96` contains no reply wait/state; inbound server rekey is private at `packet.c:1336-1369` and `transport.c:407-425,1039-1048`. Trace callbacks are unavailable in non-debug builds and are not a supported protocol API.
./LOGBOOK.md:1015:- ROUTING: Do not report these gates green or build a mock-only partial adapter. Recommend a separately reviewed minimal fork extension exposing typed server-KEX lifecycle plus a nonblocking reply-correlated global-request/keepalive API; alternatives are explicitly failing the libssh2 candidate or revising the mandatory contract. Exact options and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:1017:### 1115 — Minimal libssh2 public client-rekey fork clears the adapter blocker (TASK-260720-3vwls7)
./LOGBOOK.md:1018:- DECISION: Pinned libssh2 `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` carries one allowlisted two-file patch: `include/libssh2.h` publicly declares `libssh2_session_rekey`, and `src/session.c` adds a null-checked, nonblocking-friendly `BLOCK_ADJUST` wrapper over the existing `ssh2_kex_exchange(session, 1, &session->startup_key_state)`. No crypto, algorithm, packet, channel, or private-header surface changed.
./LOGBOOK.md:1020:- VERIFICATION: The public-header-only C integration test drove the wrapper through three `EAGAIN` results, observed client-initiated NEWKEYS installation, then forced OpenSSH `RekeyLimit` server-initiated NEWKEYS and proved post-rekey channels remained usable. Fork verify/rekey/source-tamper gates, Swift public-module packaging test, native seam tests, strict Swift format, 62 core tests, and `swift build` pass; `ReluxTunnelCore` remains free of libssh2/OpenSSL imports.
./LOGBOOK.md:1022:### 1030 — libssh2 adapter stops on absent public client-rekey API (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:1023:- BLOCKER: Exact pinned archive `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` reproduced SHA-256 `744ba3e9…c87c`. Public `include/libssh2.h` has no rekey operation; initial `libssh2_session_handshake` calls private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=0)`, while only inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` reaches private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=1)` from `src/packet.c`.
./LOGBOOK.md:1024:- IMPACT: Mandatory byte/time/explicit client rekey and `SSHTransport.requestRekey` cannot be implemented through the pinned public API. Calling the private symbol, exporting it in-task, replaying handshake, or reporting success without KEX would violate the task's no-patch/no-hidden-fallback boundary. Product code and partial packaging stopped before implementation.
./LOGBOOK.md:1025:- ROUTING: Recommend recording libssh2 red/failed at this pin. The alternative is a separately authorized, audited libssh2 fork/public rekey hook (or a future upstream pin followed by full re-audit). Exact evidence and tradeoffs are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_libssh2-rekey-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:1031:- ROUTING: Recommend an accepted candidate-neutral connected-socket/engine-bootstrap seam plus ReluxNIOSSH consumer-driven receive-credit and maximum-packet/intake hooks, each with deterministic tests. Exact evidence, rejected workarounds, alternatives, and resume condition are attached as `TASK-260715-1af33i_adapter-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:1033:### 1015 — Review: fork adapter-conformance seams accepted; three adapter-facing notes (TASK-260720-34d4du)
./LOGBOOK.md:1036:- FINDING: Snapshot lifecycle is sticky-correct: `SSHConnectionStateMachine`'s own snapshot resets to nil on transition to `userAuthentication`/`active`, but `NIOSSHHandler` re-reads it after every processed message while the KEX state machine still holds `NegotiationResult`, and only overwrites on non-nil (`NIOSSHHandler.swift:684`). Consequence: during a rekey the handler snapshot updates when the new `NegotiationResult` is computed (KEXINIT processing), slightly before newKeys activate.
./LOGBOOK.md:1039:### 1003 — ReluxNIOSSH fork exposes adapter-conformance seams (TASK-260720-34d4du)
./LOGBOOK.md:1059:### 0109 — Re-review: M3 evidence fixture hashes include a trailing newline (TASK-260715-2kchi0)
./LOGBOOK.md:1065:- FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.
./LOGBOOK.md:1067:- NOTE: `.serverInitiated` rekey reason semantically means "remote-initiated" (a server-role handler labels client-triggered KEX the same way); cosmetic for the client-only tunnel. `requestRekey` in a non-active state fails the promise and also fires `errorCaught` — callers should not treat pre-active requests as recoverable no-ops.
./LOGBOOK.md:1069:### 0850 — Minimal ReluxNIOSSH window/rekey fork is reviewable (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
./LOGBOOK.md:1070:- DECISION: The source fork is an independent SwiftPM package at `Dependencies/ReluxNIOSSH`, based byte-for-byte on SwiftNIO SSH `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c` before a 16-file allowlisted delta. The `NIOSSH` product/module and upstream 16 MiB/no-auto-rekey defaults remain source-compatible; `ReluxTunnelCore` has no dependency on the fork.
./LOGBOOK.md:1071:- IMPLEMENTATION: Child initial window/cap/adjustment policy is set through a normal pre-open channel option and observed through snapshots/events; returned credit is earned only after pipeline delivery. Client manual, per-direction protected-byte, and injected-monotonic-time rekeys share the existing KEX state machine, coalesce concurrent reasons/promises, reset generation counters on success, and observe server-initiated KEX without product reflection or a test-only API.
./LOGBOOK.md:1074:### 0821 — Candidate-neutral SSH conformance contract closes the shared-surface gap (TASK-260715-2ny6z4)
./LOGBOOK.md:1075:- DECISION: Both engine adapters use one serialized, type-erased `ReluxTunnelCore` boundary with mandatory raw host-key policy evaluation before credential access, bounded partial writes, independent byte-stream halves, capped observable per-channel receive credit, automatic byte/time plus explicit/server rekey, keepalive, stable errors, schema-v1 metrics/events, and privacy-safe opaque lane identity. ADR-014 remains open; unsupported mandatory behavior is a red matrix row, never a candidate-specific skip.
./LOGBOOK.md:1077:- VERIFICATION: The contract defines every operation, state, timeout/cancellation scope, error, metric, privacy rule, and E-ALGO/E-HOSTAUTH/E-CHANNELS/E-BACKPRESSURE/E-WINDOW/E-REKEY/E-KEEPALIVE/E-CANCEL/E-SCALE/E-ERRORS/E-METRICS-PRIVACY/E-INJECTION row. Three task-scoped PlantUML sources pass syntax checking and their lifecycle, pre-auth host verification, and channel/window/rekey SVG renders were visually inspected.
./LOGBOOK.md:1080:- MILESTONE: Audit accepted → `done`. Every load-bearing claim reproduced from upstream: tag→commit pins for both candidates plus all six transitive deps (ls-remote), all three archive SHA-256 and both license hashes (fresh downloads), 16 MiB window `SSHChannelMultiplexer.swift:211`, internal `_rekey()` `NIOSSHHandler.swift:511`, libssh2 window APIs and direct-tcpip hardcode `src/channel.c:384,457`, unexported reexchange `src/packet.c:1369`, version-macro anomaly, six CVE fix commits confirmed ancestors of `a343024`, GHSA advisories via API, exactly 679 libssh2 commits/12mo.
./LOGBOOK.md:1086:- FINDING: SwiftNIO SSH hardcodes each child receive-window target to 16 MiB and exposes client `_rekey()` only internally. libssh2 exposes generic per-channel initial/remaining/adjust APIs, but its direct-tcpip helper fixes the 2 MiB default and it likewise has no public client-rekey API or automatic byte/time threshold.
./LOGBOOK.md:1087:- DECISION: ADR-014 remains open. Both candidates retain identical host-key, window, sustained 5 GiB rekey, scale (through 1,000 channels), cancellation, algorithm, extension-build, memory, and physical-size gates. Unknowns are named experiments in `.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md`; no missing capability was waived.
